WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.830 --> 00:00:04.530
DAVA NEWMAN: We want to make
Earth as habitable and livable,

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:06.780
and since we're
accelerating this change,

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:10.290
we want to live in balance
with Spaceship Earth.

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:11.920
SARAH HANSEN: In
today's episode,

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:15.110
we're taking another look at
our planet from the outside in.

00:00:15.110 --> 00:00:16.860
DAVA NEWMAN: Simply
we're trying to design

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:19.500
a healthy relationship
between people, technology,

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:23.250
and our Earth systems for
sustainability of humanity

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:26.370
and all the living
things on the planet.

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:28.650
SARAH HANSEN: I'm your
host, Sarah Hansen.

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:30.600
Today on Chalk
Radio, we're talking

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.800
with Apollo Program Professor
of Astronautics Dava Newman.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.070
Professor Newman is an expert
in aerospace engineering,

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:40.710
and she's used that knowledge
to forge new perspectives

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:42.300
on climate change.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:46.590
You can check out Professor
Newman's course, 16.423J,

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:49.380
Aerospace Biomedical and
Life Support Engineering,

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:54.080
on OCW as well as her
climate-focused resources.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:56.060
In short, Professor
Newman has worked

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:59.510
on some very cool things,
including better spacesuits

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:02.750
for astronauts, ones designed
for much longer journeys

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:04.186
into space in the future.

00:01:04.186 --> 00:01:05.269
DAVA NEWMAN: In research--

00:01:05.269 --> 00:01:08.570
I specialize in
astronaut performance

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:11.510
and biomedical
engineering as well.

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:15.650
I spend a lot of time thinking
about humanity becoming

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:18.650
interplanetary literally when
we'll have humans going back

00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:21.740
to the moon and Mars,
and design of suits,

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:23.750
and life support
systems, and experiments

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:25.430
to keep our astronauts
healthy and well

00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:31.250
during long-duration flight,
and about 50% of my research

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:33.650
is now in climate.

00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:37.860
And I kind of frame
that as Earth systems.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:40.200
As an engineer myself
looking at complex systems,

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.120
well, there's no
more complex system

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:43.680
I submit than Earth
systems, and that

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:49.548
includes oceans to land to
air, all the way to space.

00:01:49.548 --> 00:01:51.840
SARAH HANSEN: Because of her
background as an aerospace

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.870
engineer, Professor Newman
takes a unique approach

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:56.400
to climate change.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:59.430
Many of her teachings draw on
this approach and the lessons

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:01.290
from her time at NASA.

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:03.780
DAVA NEWMAN: I served as
NASA Deputy Administrator,

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:06.630
so that's the number two
during the Obama administration

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:09.960
from 2015 to 2017.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:12.780
And most people think of NASA
and think of human spaceflight.

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:15.150
That's my research
specialty and background,

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:17.460
but really, it's
all the aeronautics,

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:20.940
breakthrough research as well,
a huge amount of Earth science

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:22.403
research related to climate.

00:02:22.403 --> 00:02:23.820
We have a hundred
science missions

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:26.970
flying at any one time,
exploring the solar system

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:28.830
and beyond, exoplanets.

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:31.470
NASA's always been
my agency, but to be

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:34.140
able to be a leader
at NASA and also join

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:37.140
the civil servants-- that
was really quite an honor,

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:40.195
and it's definitely
changed my research.

00:02:40.195 --> 00:02:41.820
And what we're talking
about, climate--

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:45.750
I came back to MIT as the Apollo
professor and said, you know,

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:48.480
what's the most important thing
I can be working on every day?

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:51.060
And my answer changed
after my NASA experience.

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:54.570
Now the answer is climate and
Earth systems, secondarily,

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:57.700
getting people to Mars.

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:00.160
SARAH HANSEN: Part of her
work with Earth systems,

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:03.760
or systems that span our globe
and connect us as a planet,

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:07.790
is helping people understand
this more global perspective.

00:03:07.790 --> 00:03:09.880
Professor Newman has
some powerful insights

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:11.890
about organizing
people around this kind

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:13.660
of big-picture thinking.

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:16.870
Her time at NASA coordinating
international efforts

00:03:16.870 --> 00:03:20.470
informs her approach to
climate action and research.

00:03:20.470 --> 00:03:22.090
DAVA NEWMAN: As an
aerospace engineer,

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:25.408
we have a pretty specific, I
think, special perspective.

00:03:25.408 --> 00:03:26.950
It's called the
overview perspective.

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:30.310
So anyone who is an aerospace
engineer-- we're always

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:33.460
looking at data from
satellites and from space,

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:36.610
first the human experience in
space, when the astronaut is

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:39.580
in low Earth orbit, looking down
on Earth, Spaceship Earth as I

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:40.315
call it.

00:03:40.315 --> 00:03:42.470
And so we're all on this
Spaceship Earth together.

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:42.970
Guess what.

00:03:42.970 --> 00:03:43.990
We're all the crew.

00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:45.880
We're actually all
astronauts because we're

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:47.530
orbiting around our sun.

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:51.250
And that's how I think we
need to think about climate

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:54.460
and accelerating positive change
is a-- it's a global issue.

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:58.390
It's an Earth-wide issue, and
I think the overview effect--

00:03:58.390 --> 00:04:01.540
you can really look at down
on Earth, get into the data,

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:04.930
but equally as important is
the human spirit and thinking

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:06.250
about collaboration.

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:09.130
It's not a nation-state
issue at all.

00:04:09.130 --> 00:04:12.100
We want to make Earth as
habitable and livable,

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:14.380
and since we're
accelerating this change,

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:18.683
we want to live in balance
with the Spaceship Earth.

00:04:18.683 --> 00:04:20.350
SARAH HANSEN: Recently,
Professor Newman

00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:25.240
co-founded the non-profit
organization called EarthDNA.

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:29.560
It's a global platform for
climate advocacy and action.

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:32.860
EarthDNA works to inform and
facilitate climate action

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:35.710
by first sharing knowledge
about our planet in a way that's

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:39.130
available and
accessible to everyone.

00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:42.190
The spirit of teaching
openly and collaboratively

00:04:42.190 --> 00:04:44.560
is one that Professor
Newman and her EarthDNA

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:48.200
co-founder, Guillermo
Trotti, care about deeply.

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:51.655
DAVA NEWMAN: We call
EarthDNA our life project.

00:04:51.655 --> 00:04:56.140
It's open source, and we really
envision a platform for climate

00:04:56.140 --> 00:04:57.370
advocacy and action.

00:04:57.370 --> 00:05:00.010
It starts with
knowledge and education.

00:05:00.010 --> 00:05:01.570
I've been an educator
my whole life,

00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:03.815
so it starts with the
educational piece.

00:05:03.815 --> 00:05:05.440
Using a lot of
artificial intelligence.

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:08.290
I call it curating the
data, petabytes of data,

00:05:08.290 --> 00:05:10.840
literally data from
our satellites,

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:12.310
so we have so much data.

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:15.490
That gets overwhelming, even for
me as an engineer and scientist

00:05:15.490 --> 00:05:18.072
but for sure for someone
who's busy working,

00:05:18.072 --> 00:05:20.530
and they just want to know a
little bit more about climate.

00:05:20.530 --> 00:05:22.278
We have to-- I call
it-- you curate

00:05:22.278 --> 00:05:24.070
that data, use our
artificial intelligence,

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:26.170
use these wonderful
visualizations so people

00:05:26.170 --> 00:05:27.700
say, ah, I get it.

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:28.930
I get temperature change.

00:05:28.930 --> 00:05:31.060
I get ocean change.

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:34.700
The hard piece, the second piece
is changing human behavior.

00:05:34.700 --> 00:05:36.933
So the point about EarthDNA--

00:05:36.933 --> 00:05:39.100
simply we're trying to
design a healthy relationship

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:42.460
between people, technology,
and our Earth systems

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:45.880
for sustainability of
humanity and all the living

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:47.005
things on the planet.

00:05:47.005 --> 00:05:48.215
We can't waste a second.

00:05:48.215 --> 00:05:49.090
We can't waste a day.

00:05:49.090 --> 00:05:52.660
It's so urgent in the next
decade to do, I think,

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:55.210
whatever all of we
can through all of us

00:05:55.210 --> 00:05:57.310
to really move the bar,
to really accelerate

00:05:57.310 --> 00:06:00.250
positive change for the Earth.

00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:03.400
SARAH HANSEN: Part of what makes
EarthDNA unique and uniquely

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:07.720
accessible is its focus on
meeting people where they are,

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:09.040
literally.

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:11.020
DAVA NEWMAN: We can talk
about the temperature,

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:12.728
global temperature,
and things like that,

00:06:12.728 --> 00:06:15.760
but I think we care
about our own zip code.

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:17.630
We care about our
family and friends.

00:06:17.630 --> 00:06:21.070
So what I care about is what's
happening in my backyard here.

00:06:21.070 --> 00:06:24.340
I have family in Montana and
California on the West Coast.

00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:26.030
We have relatives in Argentina.

00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:29.753
So I have those five zip codes,
and what I care about is, oh,

00:06:29.753 --> 00:06:30.670
what's happening here?

00:06:30.670 --> 00:06:33.170
Maybe what can I
do in those places?

00:06:33.170 --> 00:06:37.060
So the point being that we need
to have global information.

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:39.700
We need to zoom down
to maybe regional,

00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:41.910
and then we need to
care about local.

00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:44.410
And so maybe you love turtles.

00:06:44.410 --> 00:06:47.110
Maybe you love things
out in the ocean.

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:50.050
We're trying to be a network
that can connect these things

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:52.960
and give you news that
might be relevant to you.

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:55.240
What's happening
tomorrow, Saturday?

00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:56.987
Maybe I can go
join some friends,

00:06:56.987 --> 00:06:59.320
and we can do a little bit
of a beach cleanup, something

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:01.060
like that, just
something simple that I

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:03.520
enjoy doing, but
trying to connect

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:07.088
people that are like-minded.

00:07:07.088 --> 00:07:09.130
SARAH HANSEN: Professor
Newman and her colleagues

00:07:09.130 --> 00:07:11.380
have developed another
powerful strategy

00:07:11.380 --> 00:07:14.830
for helping people learn and
engage around climate issues.

00:07:14.830 --> 00:07:18.207
They've separated Earth into
four different sub-systems.

00:07:18.207 --> 00:07:20.290
DAVA NEWMAN: There's no
silver bullet for climate.

00:07:20.290 --> 00:07:23.840
It's really all these complex
systems working together,

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:26.680
so the oceans first and
foremost because the oceans

00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:30.010
are our largest sub-system
and then the land,

00:07:30.010 --> 00:07:35.150
where most people live, on land,
and then from the land, air,

00:07:35.150 --> 00:07:35.820
and then space.

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:37.730
I count space, again,
back to my roots.

00:07:37.730 --> 00:07:39.040
I'm biased.

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:40.240
I'm an aerospace engineer.

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:42.323
But that's really where
so much of the information

00:07:42.323 --> 00:07:45.460
is coming from, near space,
so our satellites, our eyes

00:07:45.460 --> 00:07:49.000
on Earth, the information
that we can see from space

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:51.770
is when we get that
global, that holistic view.

00:07:51.770 --> 00:07:55.870
So that's why I put those
four sub-systems there.

00:07:55.870 --> 00:07:58.150
We need work in
all of them, but I

00:07:58.150 --> 00:08:01.870
think I find it helpful to talk
about those four sub-systems

00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:04.163
because someone might
have affinity-- oh, for me

00:08:04.163 --> 00:08:04.830
it's the oceans.

00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:06.250
Oh, no, for me it's the land.

00:08:06.250 --> 00:08:08.590
And that's kind of
the fundamental part

00:08:08.590 --> 00:08:09.580
of the mission.

00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:14.427
It's to make the world of
work for 100% of humanity.

00:08:14.427 --> 00:08:16.510
SARAH HANSEN: An important
part of climate science

00:08:16.510 --> 00:08:18.670
is making models that
help us predict what's

00:08:18.670 --> 00:08:20.720
likely to happen in the future.

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:23.020
This posed a particularly
complex challenge

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:25.810
for Professor Newman
and the EarthDNA team.

00:08:25.810 --> 00:08:28.150
How to convey complex
climate models that

00:08:28.150 --> 00:08:31.390
draw on tons of data points
and variables to those of us

00:08:31.390 --> 00:08:33.880
who don't have a background
in climate science?

00:08:33.880 --> 00:08:35.440
DAVA NEWMAN: We
have so much data

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:38.500
from our satellites
for Earth systems.

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:40.870
Maybe just even take
ocean data, if you will.

00:08:40.870 --> 00:08:43.840
Take a look at, well, what's the
sea level temperature, what's

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:47.980
the sea level rise,
coverage of the whole Earth.

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:50.800
And then, again, we want to zoom
down into something regional.

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:52.540
Let's say the North Atlantic.

00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:54.310
We can look from,
say, New England,

00:08:54.310 --> 00:08:58.030
where we are at MIT, all the
way, then, across the Atlantic,

00:08:58.030 --> 00:09:02.210
taking all that data, going
deep into reducing it.

00:09:02.210 --> 00:09:05.440
And it can generate satellite
images from the future.

00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:11.160
Imagine I could put
that in your hand.

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:16.180
Perhaps if we immerse people
in these future visions or--

00:09:16.180 --> 00:09:20.140
boom, teleport yourself to
anywhere you want in the world.

00:09:20.140 --> 00:09:22.140
And so it's the
the storytelling,

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:24.270
and the storyline, I
think, hopefully also

00:09:24.270 --> 00:09:26.940
will engage people and
get people involved.

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:29.668
And hopefully people can
ask questions and people can

00:09:29.668 --> 00:09:30.960
think about, OK, what can I do?

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:32.460
And we can serve
up a whole bunch

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:34.470
of different opportunities,
different actions

00:09:34.470 --> 00:09:35.462
that they could take.

00:09:35.462 --> 00:09:37.170
Even though a lot of
this is open source,

00:09:37.170 --> 00:09:38.560
it's really hard to access.

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:39.550
It takes a lot of time.

00:09:39.550 --> 00:09:42.907
It can take an hour of digging,
let's say, on a NASA website

00:09:42.907 --> 00:09:44.490
to find what you
might be looking for.

00:09:44.490 --> 00:09:47.370
Well, wouldn't that be neat
if you could just speak to me,

00:09:47.370 --> 00:09:48.745
and give me a
couple of keywords,

00:09:48.745 --> 00:09:50.412
and say what you'd
want, and then, boom,

00:09:50.412 --> 00:09:51.270
I'd serve it up now.

00:09:51.270 --> 00:09:52.540
We don't have that
capability yet,

00:09:52.540 --> 00:09:53.920
but that's what
we're working on.

00:09:53.920 --> 00:09:55.128
We want to make it that easy.

00:10:00.295 --> 00:10:01.920
SARAH HANSEN: For
this episode, we also

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:04.950
had the chance to ask Professor
Newman some of your questions

00:10:04.950 --> 00:10:06.750
that you submitted to us.

00:10:06.750 --> 00:10:08.550
One of the questions was this--

00:10:08.550 --> 00:10:11.520
does the Earth have
global climate cycles,

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:16.080
and is climate change possibly
just part of a normal cycle?

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:18.300
DAVA NEWMAN: Earth
does have cycles.

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:23.220
Earth is 4.5 billion
years old, and so we've

00:10:23.220 --> 00:10:26.760
had weather and climate through
those billions and billions

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:27.520
of years.

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:31.080
So there are cycles,
but no, we are not

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:35.050
in a normal cycle by any
stretch of the imagination.

00:10:35.050 --> 00:10:38.310
This is an incredibly
accelerated response

00:10:38.310 --> 00:10:41.370
since the Industrial Revolution.

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:45.930
We actually can calculate the
percentage of climate that

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:48.382
is human-caused human-induced.

00:10:48.382 --> 00:10:50.340
There's some tools out
there, some simulations.

00:10:50.340 --> 00:10:51.007
You can imagine.

00:10:51.007 --> 00:10:52.560
Well, what if I use
this much energy?

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:54.750
What if we emit this
much carbon dioxide?

00:10:54.750 --> 00:10:57.132
Oh, what if we cut down by 50%?

00:10:57.132 --> 00:10:59.340
And what we're striving for
is, where's that balance?

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:02.310
Where's that balance for
people having healthy,

00:11:02.310 --> 00:11:05.340
wonderful, again,
economically successful

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:10.380
lives but just always trying to
keep that in mind, our effects

00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:12.720
on climate, and how
we can change things,

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:15.370
and how we can do much better.

00:11:15.370 --> 00:11:18.150
SARAH HANSEN: Another one
of your questions was this--

00:11:18.150 --> 00:11:20.730
how is climate change
transforming people's lives

00:11:20.730 --> 00:11:23.760
and societies at a broad level?

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:27.370
DAVA NEWMAN: Climate change
impacts every single one of us,

00:11:27.370 --> 00:11:28.830
and it's going to
get more acute.

00:11:28.830 --> 00:11:30.900
And it's going to
get more intense.

00:11:30.900 --> 00:11:34.480
Globally, we definitely see it,
immigration and people moving.

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:36.480
And people don't ever
want to leave their house.

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:38.890
You want to stay with
your family and friends,

00:11:38.890 --> 00:11:41.940
but if you're forced to,
especially by natural disaster,

00:11:41.940 --> 00:11:45.090
but especially by war--
but I think that we really

00:11:45.090 --> 00:11:49.740
need to show people the
connections between climate

00:11:49.740 --> 00:11:50.580
and health.

00:11:50.580 --> 00:11:52.950
So again, that's
really important today.

00:11:52.950 --> 00:11:53.730
Let's link.

00:11:53.730 --> 00:11:54.960
We have the climate data.

00:11:54.960 --> 00:11:57.593
We have health data,
but let's link them

00:11:57.593 --> 00:11:59.010
because they're
very, very linked.

00:11:59.010 --> 00:12:00.120
We can talk about asthma.

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:02.700
We can talk about people's
changing health issues,

00:12:02.700 --> 00:12:06.450
and we can look at that and say,
there's a climate link there.

00:12:06.450 --> 00:12:09.540
And that's, I think, an
important discussion to have.

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:12.090
SARAH HANSEN: Another
question you submitted was,

00:12:12.090 --> 00:12:13.860
what are one or
two of the biggest

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:17.220
challenges that governments and
concerned citizens of Spaceship

00:12:17.220 --> 00:12:20.490
Earth should try to solve in
order to prevent or mitigate

00:12:20.490 --> 00:12:21.983
climate change?

00:12:21.983 --> 00:12:23.400
DAVA NEWMAN: I
love that question,

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:26.380
and my answer is really just
a discussion with that person.

00:12:26.380 --> 00:12:28.080
What do you want to do?

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:30.000
Because I think it's
important that people

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:31.110
go with their passion.

00:12:31.110 --> 00:12:32.185
There's so much to do.

00:12:32.185 --> 00:12:33.810
There's something
that everyone can do,

00:12:33.810 --> 00:12:36.280
so I'd like to ask
it back answer,

00:12:36.280 --> 00:12:39.030
not to not answer question, but
just to know a little bit more

00:12:39.030 --> 00:12:41.880
about that person and
say, what do you like?

00:12:41.880 --> 00:12:43.410
What's really your passion?

00:12:43.410 --> 00:12:45.915
And then if I know
that, again, it

00:12:45.915 --> 00:12:47.370
could be something
for the oceans.

00:12:47.370 --> 00:12:49.200
Maybe it's something
for animals,

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:50.700
or land, or something like that.

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:52.250
Maybe it's pollution
and plastics.

00:12:52.250 --> 00:12:53.860
So good, we love a challenge.

00:12:53.860 --> 00:12:56.140
Well, what's the
plastic in the future?

00:12:56.140 --> 00:12:59.000
What's a synthetic biological--

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:02.910
what's the biological new
material-- not plastic,

00:13:02.910 --> 00:13:05.630
but new material-- in the
future that acts like plastic?

00:13:05.630 --> 00:13:07.380
We probably couldn't
live without plastic,

00:13:07.380 --> 00:13:08.490
but let's reinvent it.

00:13:08.490 --> 00:13:12.100
Let's reinvent it so that
is completely sustainable.

00:13:12.100 --> 00:13:14.460
And there's research
going on that right now,

00:13:14.460 --> 00:13:16.290
but hopefully that's
just an example

00:13:16.290 --> 00:13:19.980
for someone for that question,
seeing what they like to do

00:13:19.980 --> 00:13:22.620
and what they'd like
to get involved in.

00:13:22.620 --> 00:13:25.170
SARAH HANSEN: And our last
question from listeners--

00:13:25.170 --> 00:13:28.260
how long do we have before
it's too late to do something

00:13:28.260 --> 00:13:29.795
about climate change?

00:13:29.795 --> 00:13:31.170
DAVA NEWMAN: I'd
like to rephrase

00:13:31.170 --> 00:13:34.920
that a little bit and say,
now is the time to act.

00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:37.500
No more excuses, no
more pushing this off

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:40.688
onto the other generation.

00:13:40.688 --> 00:13:43.230
Everyone can do something today,
and these are little things.

00:13:43.230 --> 00:13:45.720
These really are not
inconvenient things.

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:47.790
These are things that I
think everyone actually

00:13:47.790 --> 00:13:51.300
would feel good about, so
the time to act is now.

00:14:00.052 --> 00:14:01.510
SARAH HANSEN: Thanks
for joining us

00:14:01.510 --> 00:14:03.820
for another episode
of Chalk Radio.

00:14:03.820 --> 00:14:06.340
We hope you're feeling
inspired and motivated to start

00:14:06.340 --> 00:14:09.070
taking some steps to
mitigate climate change based

00:14:09.070 --> 00:14:11.620
on your own passions
and interests.

00:14:11.620 --> 00:14:13.870
Don't forget to check out
Professor Newman's courses

00:14:13.870 --> 00:14:16.820
and her EarthDNA
resources on our website.

00:14:16.820 --> 00:14:20.290
You can also visit earthdna.org.

00:14:20.290 --> 00:14:22.600
Signing off from
Spaceship Earth,

00:14:22.600 --> 00:14:27.070
I'm Sarah Hansen from
MIT OpenCourseWare.

00:14:27.070 --> 00:14:31.210
Hey, before we go, did that
OCW has a birthday coming up?

00:14:31.210 --> 00:14:33.340
We're turning 20 in April.

00:14:33.340 --> 00:14:36.280
For two decades we've worked
to share MIT course materials

00:14:36.280 --> 00:14:37.330
with the world.

00:14:37.330 --> 00:14:40.720
Your support helps us to
do so freely and openly

00:14:40.720 --> 00:14:44.170
and helps make resources
like this podcast possible.

00:14:44.170 --> 00:14:46.180
If you're able to
give, please consider

00:14:46.180 --> 00:14:51.400
donating to OCW at
ocw.mit.edu so we can stay

00:14:51.400 --> 00:14:53.600
open for the next 20 years.

00:14:53.600 --> 00:14:54.100
Thank you.

00:14:57.480 --> 00:14:59.500
